## Why This Is Asked
This question tests designing a scalable, resilient streaming pipeline on GCP with real-time per-tenant isolation, schema evolution handling, and robust rollback and test strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Dataflow streaming with schema evolution handling
- Pub/Sub DLQ and idempotent writes
- BigQuery per-tenant partitions and retention policies
- IAM least privilege, cross-project boundaries
- Observability with Cloud Logging/Monitoring
- Rollback, backfill, and validation testing

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateEvent(evt) {
  // basic schema checks and type guarantees
  if (!evt.tenant_id || !evt.event_type) {
    throw new Error('Missing required fields');
  }
  return {
    ...evt,
    processed_at: new Date().toISOString(),
    schema_version: evt.version || 'v1'
  };
}
```

## Architecture Overview
- **Ingestion**: Pub/Sub topics with tenant-level filtering
- **Processing**: Dataflow with autoscaling and preemptible workers
- **Storage**: BigQuery with per-tenant partitioning and time-based retention
- **Failure Handling**: Dead-letter queues with automated retry logic
- **Monitoring**: Real-time alerts and comprehensive logging

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end design for tenant isolation, scalability, and disaster readiness in GCP.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation via per-tenant topics, datasets, and IAM bindings
- Streaming ingestion with Dataflow and Pub/Sub
- Schema registry and drift detection in Cloud Storage
- Private connectivity (PSC) to BigQuery; VPC Service Controls
- Observability, cost-aware ops, and multi-region DR

## Code Example
